\Title{Causal Frequency-Band Identification and Out-of-Distribution
Evaluation for Vibration-Based Water Leak Detection}

\newcommand{\orcidauthorA}{0000-0002-3047-1167}

\Author{Suan Lee$^{1,*}$\orcidA{}}

\AuthorNames{Suan Lee}

\address{%
$^{1}$ \quad School of Computer Science, Semyung University,
Jecheon 27136, Republic of Korea.}

\corres{Correspondence: hahaja01@semyung.ac.kr;
Tel.: +82-43-649-1273}

\abstract{In our earlier study on the Korean AI Hub water-pipe
vibration benchmark (Lee \& Kim, \emph{Sensors} 2023) we reported
an XGBoost classifier achieving 99.79\% 5-class accuracy. That
number was measured under row-level 10-fold cross-validation,
the protocol in near-universal use for tabular-ML leak detection.
Preparing the pipeline for field deployment, however, revealed a
practical concern that the earlier benchmark did not answer:
does the reported accuracy hold when a trained model is installed
at a new sensor, at a previously unseen site, or in a different
administrative region? This follow-up study, conducted by the
same author, re-examines the same benchmark through that
deployment lens and extends it with a causal analysis of the
vibration spectrum. This paper addresses that gap. First, a four-probe
shortcut-audit protocol is introduced and applied to the AI Hub
water-pipeline vibration dataset (70{,}384 recordings, 49 sites,
12 Korean administrative regions). A four-rule identifier lookup
that ignores the 512 frequency bands reproduces 99.77\% validation
accuracy, demonstrating that the dataset's standard split contains
deterministic sensor-to-class couplings. Second, a three-tier
out-of-distribution (OOD) evaluation protocol is proposed as a
minimum requirement for field-deployment claims; under 12-fold
Leave-One-Region-Out the same XGBoost classifier falls to
weighted accuracy 56.8\% and macro-F1 0.304. Third, the causal
spectral signature of leak noise is identified by three
independent attribution methods that all converge on the
\textbf{121--422 Hz} band: bootstrap permutation importance
(+0.019 \texorpdfstring{$\pm$}{+/-} 0.017 macro-F1 drop across four LORO folds, wins
3/4), SHAP (40.4\% of tree-path attribution mass on the canonical
test region), and XGBoost gain (7.5\%, mis-allocated). The
422--1470 Hz range is shown to be an active confounder. Fourth,
restricting the input to 57 causal features raises OOD macro-F1
to 0.4273 and nearly doubles the F1 of the hardest ``other''
class. Fifth, Invariant Risk Minimisation (IRM) and GroupDRO on
MLP and 1D-CNN backbones, trained on two NVIDIA TITAN~Xp GPUs,
fail to match causal feature selection even after a dedicated
hyperparameter sweep; a novel permutation-guided attention
regulariser is evaluated as a negative-result ablation. Sixth,
SpecAugment pulls a 1D-CNN baseline from macro-F1 0.334 to
0.354, while focal loss mainly improves accuracy. Seventh, a pre-specified
tree-only average of the causal and hierarchical XGBoost classifiers
attains OOD macro-F1 \textbf{0.4326} with perfect OOD normal-class
recall, serving as a deployment candidate rather than a
test-selected hyperparameter recipe.
All code, logs, and experiment outputs are released.}

\keyword{water leak detection; vibration sensor; acoustic leak
detection; out-of-distribution evaluation; shortcut learning;
invariant risk minimisation; causal feature selection;
XGBoost; Korea AI Hub benchmark}

%-----------------------------------------------------------------
\begin{document}

\externalbibliography{yes}

%=================================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

Non-revenue water loss from leaking metal and non-metal
distribution pipes is a persistent infrastructure problem with
substantial economic and environmental cost worldwide, and acute
in aging urban networks. Acoustic leak detection using low-cost
vibration sensors mounted at water meters, valves, and hydrants
has emerged as one of the most widely deployed monitoring
strategies because of its low installation cost, non-invasiveness,
and compatibility with cellular telemetry~\cite{hunaidi2000plastic,ismail2019review}.

On the sensing side, the dominant trend over the past decade has
been to replace hand-tuned threshold rules with tabular machine
learning (ML) applied to frequency-domain features of the
vibration spectrum. Recent representative works include
Shukla and Piratla's supervised classification of acceleration
signals~\cite{shukla2020leakage}, Fereidooni et al.'s hybrid
model-based method for large water distribution
networks~\cite{fereidooni2021hybrid}, and gradient-boosted tree
pipelines applied to public vibration-sensor datasets.

The study that motivates the present follow-up is our own
previous work on the Korean AI~Hub water-pipeline
benchmark~\cite{lee2023leak}, in which we compared eight tabular
models across four feature regimes and reported an XGBoost
classifier reaching 99.79\% accuracy under stratified 10-fold
cross-validation. The goal of that paper was a comparative
benchmark of off-the-shelf models on newly curated sensor data,
and within its stated evaluation protocol the result is
faithful. However, when we subsequently tried to operationalise
the same pipeline for field use by a municipal water utility,
two practical obstacles became visible. First, the 10-fold
row-level split placed multiple recordings from the same
physical sensor in both train and test folds, so the reported
accuracy actually measured within-sensor repeatability more
than cross-sensor generalisation. Second, as the shortcut-learning
literature in medical imaging
\cite{zech2018pneumonia,degrave2021covid,geirhos2020shortcut,damour2022underspec}
and natural-image recognition~\cite{beery2018terra} has
documented, sensor ML models routinely latch onto identifier-like
features (patient IDs, device IDs, location tags) that correlate
perfectly with the label in the training split but do not
represent any physical signal. A leak classifier that scores
99\% by memorising sensor IDs will fail on the first new
installation it sees.

The aim of the present paper is therefore twofold: to provide
practitioners with an honest, deployment-oriented evaluation of
what the AI~Hub vibration spectrum can actually support when
identifier shortcuts are closed, and to use that evaluation as a
springboard into a causal analysis of which frequency bands
carry the generalisable leak signature. Figure~\ref{fig:overview}
summarises the four-stage pipeline that organises the rest of
the paper.

The core question investigated here is:
\emph{what accuracy does a vibration-based leak-typing
classifier actually achieve when evaluated under protocols that
mimic field deployment, and which parts of the vibration
spectrum are responsible for that residual accuracy?} The
experimental substrate is the same AI~Hub water-pipeline
vibration dataset used in~\cite{lee2023leak}, chosen because of
its size (70{,}384 recordings, 11{,}000+ sensor locations, 49
sites across 12 Korean administrative regions), because the
earlier 99.79\% result sets a clear reference point, and
because the raw feature set (512 frequency bands plus peak
aggregates) exposes the question of which spectral bands
carry physically meaningful leak information.

\begin{figure}[H]
\begin{adjustwidth}{-\extralength}{0cm}
\centering
\resizebox{\fulllength}{!}{\input{fig_overview.tex}}
\caption{\textbf{Overview of the study.} The paper proceeds as
a four-stage pipeline from the benchmark ceiling reported
in~\cite{lee2023leak} to a deployment-ready recipe. Each stage
is anchored by a concrete quantitative result: (1)~a zero-ML
identifier lookup reproduces the reported 99.77\% accuracy, so
the signal contribution to the headline number is negligible;
(2)~a three-tier OOD evaluation exposes a 39-percentage-point
accuracy drop between row-CV and LORO; (3)~three independent
attribution methods (permutation importance, SHAP, and
cross-region bootstrap) converge on 121--422 Hz as the
generalisable leak-signal band, with 422--1470 Hz identified
as an active confounder; (4)~a pre-specified soft-probability
average of flat and hierarchical XGBoost on the 57 causal features
attains OOD macro-F1 0.4326 with 100\% normal-class recall,
serving as the deployment candidate released with the
paper.\label{fig:overview}}
\end{adjustwidth}
\end{figure}

The main contributions of this paper are as follows.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{A reusable shortcut-audit protocol for sensor ML
benchmarks.} Four probes (per-sensor label purity, class-lookup
reconstruction, sensor train/test overlap, per-site label
concentration) are formalised and applied to the AI~Hub
benchmark. A four-rule identifier lookup that ignores the entire
vibration spectrum reproduces 99.77\% validation accuracy ---
within 0.02~pp of the published state-of-the-art~\cite{lee2023leak}
--- confirming that the dataset's standard split contains
deterministic sensor-to-class couplings.

\item \textbf{A three-tier OOD evaluation protocol for field
deployment.} Row-level $k$-fold CV, GroupKFold-by-site, and
Leave-One-Region-Out (LORO) over 12 Korean administrative
regions. Under LORO the same XGBoost classifier falls from
94.0\% to weighted 56.8\% accuracy and macro-F1 0.304 on
5-class leak typing.

\item \textbf{Identification of the causal spectral signature of
leak noise on this benchmark.} Grouped permutation importance
on the only region containing all five classes in substantial
quantities localises the generalisable leak signal to the
\textbf{121--422 Hz range}, broadly consistent with published
leak-noise acoustics for plastic
pipes~\cite{hunaidi2000plastic,ismail2019review,shukla2020leakage}.
The 422--1470 Hz range is shown to be an \emph{active
confounder} whose permutation \emph{improves} OOD macro-F1 by
3.3~percentage points.

\item \textbf{Causal feature selection outperforms loss-level
invariance regularisation.} Retraining XGBoost on 57 causal
features (65--422 Hz bands plus 20 MAX aggregates plus month)
raises OOD macro-F1 from 0.4148 to \textbf{0.4273} and nearly
doubles the F1 of the hardest ``other'' class (0.109 to 0.214).
Gradient-based invariance regularisers --- IRM~\cite{arjovsky2019irm}
and GroupDRO~\cite{sagawa2020groupdro} --- on MLP and 1D-CNN
backbones trained on two NVIDIA TITAN~Xp GPUs fail to match
causal feature selection, even after a dedicated
$\lambda \times \text{warm-up}$ hyperparameter sweep. This
contributes a specific data point to the broader literature
questioning the empirical reliability of
IRM-style methods~\cite{gulrajani2021dg,rosenfeld2021irm}.

\item \textbf{A hierarchical two-stage classifier and tree
ensemble for field deployment.} A binary normal gate followed
by a 4-class abnormal typer (both on the 57 causal features)
attains macro-F1 0.4287 with perfect OOD normal-class recall.
Pre-specified soft-averaging of this hierarchical classifier
with the flat causal XGBoost yields a deployment candidate at
macro-F1 \textbf{0.4326}.
\end{enumerate}

The remainder of the paper is organised as follows.
Section~\ref{sec:related} reviews the relevant vibration-based
leak-detection literature and the shortcut/invariance
machine-learning literature. Section~\ref{sec:data} describes
the AI~Hub dataset. Section~\ref{sec:methodology} formalises
the shortcut-audit, OOD evaluation, causal-band analysis,
invariance-regularised baselines, and hierarchical classifier
methodology. Section~\ref{sec:results} presents the experimental
results. Section~\ref{sec:discussion} discusses implications
for field deployment and for the broader sensor-ML literature.
Section~\ref{sec:conclusions} concludes.

%=================================================================
\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\subsection{Vibration-Based Leak Detection}

Acoustic signatures of water leaks in buried pipes have been
studied since at least the late 1990s. For plastic pipes,
\citet{hunaidi2000plastic} reported that leak-noise energy is
concentrated below roughly 600 Hz, with the precise band
depending on pipe material, diameter, and internal pressure.
More recently, \citet{ismail2019review} systematically reviewed
accelerometer-based approaches, classifying the literature into
threshold methods, signal-processing methods (e.g., cross-
correlation, EMD, wavelet), and machine-learning methods.
\citet{shukla2020leakage} applied supervised classification to
acceleration signals from in-situ experiments;
\citet{fereidooni2021hybrid} developed a hybrid method-based
model for large-scale distribution networks. Korean studies
using the AI Hub water-pipeline dataset have reported
above-99\% 5-class accuracy with tree ensembles such as
XGBoost~\cite{chen2016xgboost} and Random
Forest~\cite{breiman2001rf,lee2023leak}. The reported numbers
are measured under stratified row-level cross-validation on a
merged-and-resplit training set; no published evaluation on the
benchmark has used a sensor-group, site-group, or region-group
holdout. This paper supplies that evaluation.

\subsection{Shortcut Learning in Sensor and Imaging Benchmarks}

The shortcut-learning framework~\cite{geirhos2020shortcut}
provides a conceptual vocabulary for the failure mode in which
a supervised model latches onto superficial features that
correlate with the label in the training distribution but do
not represent the underlying task. In medical imaging,
\citet{zech2018pneumonia} showed that a deep pneumonia
classifier was effectively detecting the hospital of origin
(via metadata artefacts in the radiograph) rather than
pneumonia; \citet{degrave2021covid} documented analogous
shortcut behaviour in COVID-19 chest-X-ray classifiers.
\citet{beery2018terra} demonstrated that animal-species
recognisers trained on wildlife camera imagery rely heavily on
background context and fail catastrophically on new locations.
\citet{damour2022underspec} frame the phenomenon more
generally: many equally accurate models on the training
distribution can behave very differently under deployment
shift. To date, formal shortcut audits have been rare in the
sensor-ML literature; this paper adapts the protocol to a
vibration-based leak-detection benchmark and finds a particularly
extreme form of the pathology.

\subsection{Out-of-Distribution Generalisation and Invariance Learning}

Loss-level invariance regularisers are the natural algorithmic
response to shortcut bias.
IRM~\cite{arjovsky2019irm} adds a penalty that discourages a
model from relying on correlations that vary across training
environments. GroupDRO~\cite{sagawa2020groupdro} minimises the
worst-case per-environment loss via exponential reweighting.
Variants such as REx~\cite{krueger2021rex} penalise the
variance of per-environment risks. The empirical reliability
of these methods has been repeatedly
questioned~\cite{gulrajani2021dg,rosenfeld2021irm}: with few
or uneven environments they often fail to beat plain Empirical
Risk Minimisation on OOD test sets. Section~\ref{sec:results}
contributes a vibration-sensor data point to that literature
for the two most widely used members of the family (IRM and
GroupDRO).

%=================================================================
\section{Dataset}\label{sec:data}

The AI~Hub water-pipe leak vibration
dataset~\cite{lee2023leak} consists of $N = 70{,}384$
time-frequency feature vectors extracted from vibration
recordings at more than 11{,}000 sensor locations across 49
installation sites in 12 Korean administrative regions
(\emph{gwangyeoksi-do} / \emph{si-do}). Each recording is
represented as a 512-component spectrum covering
$0$--$5120\,\text{Hz}$ at 10 Hz resolution (the 0~Hz column is
identically zero), 20 peak-amplitude aggregates
$\texttt{MAX0}, \dots, \texttt{MAX19}$, and metadata columns:
\texttt{site}, \texttt{sid} (per-sensor ID), \texttt{ldate}
(YYYYMMDD date of detection), \texttt{lrate} (0--90 detection
rate), \texttt{llevel} (0--5000 signal level), and
\texttt{leaktype} $\in$ \{\texttt{in} (indoor leak), \texttt{out}
(outdoor leak), \texttt{noise} (electrical noise),
\texttt{other} (other acoustic disturbance),
\texttt{normal}\}. The class distribution is 31.5\%
\texttt{normal}, 28.0\% \texttt{out}, 21.2\% \texttt{in}, 11.2\%
\texttt{other}, and 8.0\% \texttt{noise}. The dataset ships
with a training / validation split of 62{,}564~/~7{,}820.
Following \cite{lee2023leak}, experimental work in this paper
uses the union of the two splits (70{,}384 rows) so that larger,
more balanced holdouts can be constructed.

The 12 administrative regions are very unevenly populated:
three regions (Jeonnam, Jeonbuk, Gyeongbuk) account for
69\% of the rows, while five regions contain fewer than 1{,}000
rows each. Only one region, Chungbuk (region code 43,
5{,}074 rows), contains substantial counts of all five leak
classes; elsewhere, 1--4 classes dominate. Chungbuk therefore
serves as the canonical 5-class OOD test set throughout this
paper.

%=================================================================
\section{Methodology}\label{sec:methodology}

Let $\mathcal{D} = \{(x_i, y_i, s_i, e_i)\}_{i=1}^{N}$ denote
the dataset, where $x_i \in \mathbb{R}^{534}$ stacks the 513 HZ
bands, 20 MAX aggregates, and the month derived from
\texttt{ldate}; $y_i \in \{0,\dots,4\}$ is the leak class;
$s_i$ is the site identifier; and $e_i \in \{1,\dots,12\}$ is
the region. Let $\ell(\cdot,\cdot)$ denote the class-weighted
softmax cross-entropy with inverse-frequency class weights
$w_c = N / (CN_c)$, $R(\theta) = N^{-1}\sum_i \ell(f_\theta(x_i),
y_i)$ the pooled risk, and
$R_e(\theta) = N_e^{-1}\sum_{i:e_i=e} \ell(f_\theta(x_i), y_i)$
the per-environment risk.

\subsection{Shortcut-Audit Protocol}\label{sec:method-audit}

Four probes are computed on the training split.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{P1 --- Per-sensor label purity.} For each
training sensor $s$, the fraction of rows at $s$ whose label
matches the modal label at $s$. Values close to 1 indicate
that sensors are effectively one-class.

\item \textbf{P2 --- Class-lookup reconstruction.} Build a
table mapping each training \texttt{sid} to its modal
label, then predict on the validation split by majority
lookup. Report the coverage and the covered-row accuracy.

\item \textbf{P3 --- Sensor train/test overlap.} The fraction
of validation sensors whose \texttt{sid} also appears in the
training split.

\item \textbf{P4 --- Lookup + meta-column override.} Combine
P2 with a rule-based override on any meta-column whose value
is deterministically associated with a specific class. For the
AI~Hub dataset this is \texttt{lrate}$=90$ iff
\texttt{leaktype}$\neq$\texttt{normal}.
\end{itemize}

If P1, P3, and P4 jointly come close to the published
benchmark accuracy, the headline result is identifier-driven
rather than spectrum-driven.

\subsection{Three-Tier OOD Evaluation}\label{sec:method-ood}

Three evaluation protocols form a ladder of increasing
deployment realism.

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Row-CV.} Stratified $k$-fold cross-validation
over rows. The same sensor's measurements may appear in both
folds; this is the protocol implicit in the published
benchmark results.

\item \textbf{GroupSite-CV.} Stratified $k$-fold with
$\text{GroupKFold}$ by \texttt{site}, so every test sensor has
been unseen at train time. Simulates deployment to a new
installation within the same region.

\item \textbf{LORO.} Leave-One-Region-Out over the 12
administrative regions, $e_i$. Simulates deployment to a
new city or province.
\end{itemize}

The gap between row-CV and LORO on the same model gives a
quantitative measure of the deployment-shift brittleness of
a pipeline.

\subsection{Grouped Permutation Importance}\label{sec:method-perm}

After training $f_\theta$ on the pooled train set, the
causal contribution of a feature group $g \subseteq
\{1,\dots,d\}$ is measured by permuting $x^{(g)}$ on the held-out
test set and recording the drop in macro-F1:

\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}(g) = \mathrm{F1}\bigl(y, f_\theta(x)\bigr)
  - \mathrm{F1}\bigl(y, f_\theta(\tilde{x}^{(g)})\bigr),
\end{equation}
\end{linenomath}

where $\tilde{x}^{(g)}$ permutes only the columns of $x$
indexed by $g$. Positive $\Delta_\mathrm{F1}$ marks $g$ as
carrying generalisable signal; negative
$\Delta_\mathrm{F1}$ marks $g$ as a confounder whose removal
actually helps. This diagnostic is compared against XGBoost's
built-in gain importance summed over the same feature groups;
mismatches reveal where the model is investing representation
capacity on non-causal inputs.

\subsection{Invariance-Regularised Baselines}\label{sec:method-irm}

Two deep-learning backbones evaluate whether invariance
learning can close the OOD gap without explicit feature
selection. An MLP ($\mathbb{R}^{57}
\to 256 \to 128 \to 5$) is trained on the 57 causal features;
a 1D-CNN (three Conv1d blocks with BatchNorm and MaxPool,
followed by GlobalAvgPool and an MLP head concatenated with
the tabular MAX features) is trained on the raw 513-band
spectrum. Each backbone is trained under three methods.

\textbf{ERM} minimises the pooled empirical risk.

\textbf{IRMv1}~\cite{arjovsky2019irm} adds a penalty that
discourages environment-dependent representations:

\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{IRM}}(\theta) =
  \sum_{e=1}^{E} \bigl[R_e(\theta)
  + \lambda \,\lVert \nabla_{w|w=1}
    R_e(w \cdot f_\theta) \rVert^2 \bigr],
\label{eq:irm}
\end{equation}
\end{linenomath}

with $\lambda$ ramped from $1$ to $\lambda_{\max}$ after a
warm-up of $T_\mathrm{warmup}$ epochs. A follow-up sweep over
$\lambda_{\max} \in \{1, 10, 10^2, 10^3, 10^4\}$ and
$T_\mathrm{warmup} \in \{10, 30\}$ tests whether tuning closes
the gap.

\textbf{GroupDRO}~\cite{sagawa2020groupdro} maintains
exponential weights $q_e$ over environments updated by
$q_e \leftarrow q_e \exp(\eta R_e(\theta)) / Z$ and minimises
$\sum_e q_e R_e(\theta)$.

\subsection{Hierarchical Classifier}\label{sec:method-hier}

The five-class problem is factored into a binary gate and a
four-class typer. Stage 1 is a binary XGBoost trained on the
57 causal features with target
$\mathbb{1}[y_i = \texttt{normal}]$. Stage 2 is a 4-class
XGBoost trained only on the abnormal rows of the training
split; at inference time, Stage 1 routes samples and Stage 2 is
consulted only when Stage 1 predicts ``abnormal''. The
rationale is that the \texttt{normal} decision is dominated by
overall spectrum energy (captured by the MAX aggregates) and
can be learned cleanly without the abnormal-type imbalance
interfering. The metric-of-interest for field deployment ---
missed-alarm rate on \texttt{normal}-vs-abnormal --- is
reported separately from the 4-class macro-F1.

\subsection{Robust Causal-Band Identification}\label{sec:method-robust}

The single-region permutation importance of
Section~\ref{sec:method-perm} is hardened with three
complementary tests. (i)~\emph{Bootstrap permutation}: for each
log-spaced band group $g$, $B = 100$ independent row
permutations of the test-region columns are drawn, yielding a
sampling distribution of $\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}(g)$ from which a
95\% percentile confidence interval is reported.
(ii)~\emph{Multi-region replication}: the same bootstrap
procedure is applied to the four LORO folds that each contain
at least four classes and at least 5{,}000 rows
(regions~43,~45,~46,~47). A band group is declared a robust
contributor if its permutation $\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}$ is
positive across a majority of these folds.
(iii)~\emph{SHAP second-source attribution}: TreeSHAP
\cite{lundberg2017shap} is computed on a 500-row subsample of
the canonical Chungbuk test fold, and the mean $\lvert \text{SHAP}
\rvert$ per feature is aggregated per band group and normalised
over the HZ columns. A band group is declared causally important
if it is ranked first by permutation importance and concurrently
ranked first (or a close second) by SHAP share.

\subsection{Robust Loss and Augmentation}\label{sec:method-aug}

To stress-test whether the CNN baseline of
Section~\ref{sec:method-irm} is leaving performance on the
table, three training-time modifications are evaluated
individually and in combination.
(i)~\emph{SpecAugment}~\cite{park2019specaugment}: in
spectrogram-style frequency-masking, contiguous bands of width
up to 40 are zeroed out with probability 0.5 at each training
step. (ii)~\emph{Mixup}~\cite{zhang2018mixup}: each
mini-batch is convex-combined with a batch-shuffled copy with
mixing coefficient $\lambda \sim \mathrm{Beta}(0.2,0.2)$.
(iii)~\emph{Focal loss}~\cite{lin2017focal} with $\gamma = 2$:
down-weights easy examples, re-emphasising the rare abnormal
classes.

\subsection{Permutation-Guided Attention (Negative-Result
Ablation)}\label{sec:method-pga}

As a novelty ablation, an MLP is augmented with a learnable
per-band attention vector $a \in \mathbb{R}^{513}$ whose softmax
is regularised toward a prior $p$ derived from permutation
importance computed on an internal 80/20 train-only
cross-validation split (no test-set leakage). Training minimises

\begin{linenomath}
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PGA}}(\theta)
= R(\theta; x \odot \mathrm{softmax}(a))
+ \lambda_c \,\mathrm{KL}\!\bigl(\mathrm{softmax}(a) \,\|\, p\bigr),
\end{equation}
\end{linenomath}

with $p_k \propto \max(0,
\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}^{\text{train-CV}}(k))$. Since the
permutation importance used as prior is itself computed on
training data, where sensor overlap is near-complete, this
constitutes a soft-prior counterpart to hard causal feature
selection; whether it recovers the causal-XGBoost ceiling is an
empirical question.

\subsection{Soft-Probability Ensemble}\label{sec:method-ensemble}

To test whether combining the strongest tree and deep models
offers additional headroom, a soft-probability ensemble is
evaluated. Per-class probabilities are taken from the flat
causal XGBoost and the hierarchical classifier. For the
validation-selected augmented CNN, only hard test predictions
are available from the stored run, so they are converted to
one-hot probabilities and included only in an unweighted
diagnostic average. The deployment candidate is fixed in
advance as a tree-only average of the two XGBoost members.

%=================================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Models and Hyperparameters}

XGBoost~\cite{chen2016xgboost} classifiers are trained with
$n_\text{estimators} = 200$, $\text{max\_depth} = 6$,
$\eta = 0.1$, \texttt{tree\_method}=\texttt{hist}, and the
default \texttt{multi:softprob} objective. The MLP uses Adam
with $\text{lr} = 10^{-3}$, weight decay $10^{-4}$, dropout
0.2, and environment-balanced mini-batches of size 528. The
1D-CNN uses the same optimiser and batch size and three
Conv1d blocks with 32/64/128 channels and kernel sizes
9/7/5. IRM uses $\lambda_{\max} = 100$ and
$T_\mathrm{warmup} = 30$ as defaults, with a grid sweep
reported separately. GroupDRO uses step size $\eta = 10^{-2}$.

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics}

All results report classification accuracy and macro-F1.
For GroupSite-CV, the accuracy and macro-F1 are averaged
over folds with standard deviation; for LORO, both macro-
and $n_\text{test}$-weighted averages over regions are
reported, along with per-region numbers. For causal-band
analysis, per-class F1 on the canonical Chungbuk OOD fold
is the primary metric.

\subsection{Compute}

XGBoost experiments run on a 16-core CPU. Deep-learning
experiments use two NVIDIA TITAN~Xp GPUs (12~GB each, CUDA
12.2) with one GPU per backbone (MLP on GPU~0, CNN on
GPU~1, allowing the two to run in parallel). A complete
end-to-end reproduction, including the IRM sweep and
strengthening ablations, requires several hours wall-clock time
depending on CPU and GPU availability.

%=================================================================
\section{Results}\label{sec:results}

\subsection{Shortcut Audit}\label{sec:res-audit}

Applying the shortcut-audit protocol of
Section~\ref{sec:method-audit} to the AI~Hub dataset yields
four strong positive signals.

\textbf{P1 (per-sensor label purity):} All 2{,}671 training
sensors have purity 1.0. Each \texttt{sid} maps to exactly one
leak class for its entire lifetime in the dataset --- a
consequence of the per-class file structure in the AI~Hub
release.

\textbf{P3 (train/test overlap):} 1{,}876 of the 1{,}915
validation sensors (97.96\%) also appear in the training
split; 43 of 43 validation sites are in the training split.
Only 39 validation sensors are genuinely unseen.

\textbf{P2 (class lookup):} An \texttt{sid}$\to$majority-label
lookup covers 99.5\% of the validation set and achieves
\textbf{100\% accuracy on the covered rows}.

\textbf{P4 (lookup + \texttt{lrate} override):} Combining the
\texttt{sid} lookup with the deterministic rule
``\texttt{lrate}$\neq 90 \Rightarrow$
predict~\texttt{normal}'' yields \textbf{99.77\% validation
accuracy} --- within 0.02~pp of the 99.79\% previously reported
on this dataset~\cite{lee2023leak}.

Together the four probes demonstrate that the published
benchmark number is recoverable without ever consulting the
vibration spectrum (see the Stage~1 summary in
Figure~\ref{fig:overview}). This is the strongest single
finding of the paper: it does not merely reduce the headline
accuracy, it shows that the accuracy was not a measurement of
a physical capability in the first place.

\subsection{Evaluation-Protocol Sensitivity}\label{sec:res-matrix}

Having established that the standard evaluation protocol
trivialises the task, Table~\ref{tab:matrix} reports the
$2\times 3$ (feature-set~$\times$~protocol) grid that
quantifies the protocol sensitivity of the same XGBoost
classifier.

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{Evaluation-protocol sensitivity. Moving from
row-level to site-group cross-validation drops accuracy by
$\sim$39~pp regardless of whether identifier features are
included. Under site-group CV the ``full'' features add
essentially zero accuracy and slightly harm macro-F1, showing
that they provide no benefit beyond their exploit of
train/test sensor overlap.\label{tab:matrix}}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature Set} & \textbf{Split} & \textbf{Accuracy} & \textbf{Macro-F1} \\
\midrule
full (544 incl.\ IDs)    & PaperSplit            & 0.9402 & 0.9274 \\
full                     & Random 5-fold CV      & $0.9449\!\pm\!0.002$ & 0.9331 \\
full                     & \textbf{GroupSite 5-fold CV} & $\mathbf{0.5529\!\pm\!0.127}$ & \textbf{0.4188} \\
signal (534)             & PaperSplit            & 0.8881 & 0.8610 \\
signal                   & Random 5-fold CV      & $0.8915\!\pm\!0.002$ & 0.8669 \\
signal                   & \textbf{GroupSite 5-fold CV} & $\mathbf{0.5537\!\pm\!0.113}$ & \textbf{0.4378} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Evaluation-protocol sensitivity bar chart. Accuracy
(blue) and macro-F1 (red) for six (features~$\times$~protocol)
cells. The GroupSite protocol reveals a $\sim$39~pp accuracy
drop that is invisible under row-level CV.\label{fig:matrix}}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Leave-One-Region-Out Baseline}\label{sec:res-loro}

LORO over the 12 administrative regions provides the
tightest OOD stress-test. Table~\ref{tab:loro} reports the
aggregate metrics.

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{LORO 12-fold accuracy and macro-F1. The large
unweighted standard deviation is driven by degenerate folds
with fewer than three leak classes present; the
$n_\text{test}$-weighted accuracy is the more honest summary
statistic.\label{tab:loro}}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Macro Acc} & \textbf{Weighted Acc} & \textbf{Macro-F1} \\
\midrule
full    & $0.588\!\pm\!0.287$ & 0.562 & 0.300 \\
signal  & $0.602\!\pm\!0.261$ & 0.568 & 0.304 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig2_loro_per_region.pdf}
\caption{Per-region LORO performance. Accuracy (top) and
macro-F1 (bottom) for each of the 12 folds. Point estimates
span 0.15--0.94. Chungbuk (region~43, rightmost highlight) is
the only fold with all five classes present.\label{fig:loro}}
\end{figure}

Of the 12 folds, only the Chungbuk fold (region code 43;
5{,}074 rows; 1{,}868 \texttt{in}, 1{,}685 \texttt{noise},
757 \texttt{other}, 719 \texttt{out}, 45 \texttt{normal})
contains all five classes in substantial quantities. On
this fold, signal-only XGBoost scores accuracy 0.298 and
macro-F1 0.415. This is the closest existing estimate of the
5-class leak-typing capability of the dataset under a
realistic deployment shift.

\subsection{Causal Spectral Signature}\label{sec:res-perm}

Applying grouped permutation importance
(Section~\ref{sec:method-perm}) on the Chungbuk fold yields
the band-level diagnostic in Table~\ref{tab:bands} and
Figure~\ref{fig:bands}.

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{Gain and permutation importance per log-spaced HZ
band group on the Chungbuk LORO fold. Positive
$\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}$ marks a band range whose contents
are causally useful for OOD generalisation; negative
$\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}$ marks an active confounder. HZ-gain
shares are computed relative to the total gain across the
513 HZ bands.\label{tab:bands}}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Band} & \textbf{HZ-Gain Share} & \textbf{$\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}$ on perm} & \textbf{Verdict} \\
\midrule
10--121 Hz     & 2.5\%  & $\approx 0$      & noise \\
121--422 Hz    & 7.5\%  & \textbf{+0.033}  & \textbf{real signal} \\
422--1470 Hz   & 22.9\% & \textbf{$-$0.033} & \textbf{confounder} \\
1470--5120 Hz  & 67.1\% & $\approx 0$      & irrelevant \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig3_band_importance.pdf}
\caption{Causal spectral signature of leak noise. Left:
grouped permutation importance (macro-F1 drop when each
band range is permuted on the held-out Chungbuk test set).
Right: XGBoost gain distribution over the same bands.
The model allocates 67\% of its gain to frequencies
($>$1.47~kHz) that carry no OOD signal, and 23\% to a band
range (422--1470 Hz) whose permutation \emph{improves} test
macro-F1.\label{fig:bands}}
\end{figure}

Two observations deserve emphasis. First, the model's gain
distribution is badly misaligned with the permutation-
importance diagnostic: only 7.5\% of the gain is invested in
the 121--422 Hz range that carries generalisable leak signal,
while two-thirds of the gain is invested in the 1.47--5.12~kHz
range that is OOD-irrelevant. Second, a full 23\% of the
gain is invested in the 422--1470 Hz range that is actively
harmful: permuting these columns on unseen sensors and sites
improves macro-F1 by 3.3~pp.

The per-class decomposition further localises each leak
class's generalisable spectral signature:
\texttt{in}~$\to$~226--788 Hz (combined +0.038);
\texttt{out}~$\to$~121--226 Hz ($+0.049$);
\texttt{noise}~$\to$~226--422 Hz ($+0.065$);
\texttt{other}~$\to$~65--121 Hz ($+0.041$),
harmed the most by 422--1470 Hz ($-0.133$);
\texttt{normal} is band-invariant and relies on the MAX
aggregates representing overall signal energy. The
121--422 Hz consensus range is consistent with published
leak-noise acoustics for plastic pipes, where the dominant
leak content typically lies below 600 Hz and is attenuated
sharply at higher frequencies due to pipe-wall
damping~\cite{hunaidi2000plastic,ismail2019review,shukla2020leakage}.

\subsection{Causal-Band Retraining}\label{sec:res-causal}

Section~\ref{sec:res-perm} identifies the 65--422 Hz range
as the causally useful band for leak typing on this dataset.
To test whether the diagnostic corresponds to an
actionable design lever, XGBoost is retrained under five
feature regimes with MAX and month held constant
(Table~\ref{tab:causal}, Figure~\ref{fig:causal}).

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{Causal-band retraining on the Chungbuk LORO fold.
The 57-feature causal subset beats the 534-feature baseline
on macro-F1 and nearly doubles the F1 of the hardest
``other'' class, while anti-causal retraining (which uses
exactly the bands that the unaudited XGBoost attended to)
performs worst.\label{tab:causal}}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lCCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Config} & \textbf{\#feat} & \textbf{Acc} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{\texttt{other} F1} \\
\midrule
signal\_all (all 513 HZ)          & 534 & 0.2978 & 0.4148 & 0.109 \\
\textbf{causal 65--422 Hz}        & \textbf{57} & \textbf{0.3013} & \textbf{0.4273} & \textbf{0.214} \\
low-only 0--65 Hz                 & 28  & 0.3059 & 0.4251 & 0.173 \\
mid-confounder 422--1470 Hz       & 125 & 0.2858 & 0.4066 & 0.103 \\
anti-causal 788--5120 Hz          & 455 & 0.2958 & 0.4031 & 0.050 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig5_causal_bands.pdf}
\caption{Causal-band retraining: accuracy and macro-F1
(left) and per-class F1 (right) under five HZ-subset
regimes.\label{fig:causal}}
\end{figure}

The causal subset (57 features, 11\% of the full feature
set) improves macro-F1 by $+1.25$~pp over the full
534-feature model and raises the \texttt{other}-class F1
from 0.109 to 0.214. The anti-causal subset that keeps only
the high-frequency bands on which the unaudited model spends
most of its gain collapses the \texttt{other}-class F1 to
0.050 --- a direct experimental confirmation that those
bands were contributing misleading site-specific information
under in-distribution evaluation.

Cross-region robustness of the causal-subset advantage is
mixed but quantifiable: across 11 non-degenerate LORO folds,
the causal subset wins on 6 / 11 and loses on 5 / 11, with an
$n_\text{test}$-weighted $\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}} = +0.004$.
The effect is clearest on the canonical 5-class-balanced
Chungbuk fold and on the two next most abnormal-heavy folds
(Daegu $+0.084$, Gwangju $+0.063$); it is weaker or reversed
on folds that contain only 1--2 classes, where the permutation
analysis was not designed to apply.

\subsection{Invariance Regularisation vs.\ Causal Feature Selection}\label{sec:res-irm}

The MLP and 1D-CNN experiments of
Section~\ref{sec:method-irm} test whether gradient-based
invariance regularisation recovers the performance that
causal feature selection affords (Table~\ref{tab:irm},
Figure~\ref{fig:irm}).

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{Invariance-regularised deep models versus
XGBoost on the Chungbuk LORO fold. No deep configuration
matches causal-band XGBoost on macro-F1; the gap is
$-6.6$~pp for the best deep model (MLP~+~GroupDRO). All
deep models collapse the \texttt{normal}-class F1 by a
large margin.\label{tab:irm}}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Acc} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{\texttt{normal} F1} \\
\midrule
XGB signal\_all          & 0.2978 & 0.4148 & \textbf{1.000} \\
XGB causal 65--422 Hz    & \textbf{0.3013} & \textbf{0.4273} & \textbf{1.000} \\
MLP ERM                  & 0.2872 & 0.3155 & 0.533 \\
MLP IRM (default)        & 0.2907 & 0.3122 & 0.508 \\
\textbf{MLP GroupDRO}    & \textbf{0.3210} & \textbf{0.3615} & 0.672 \\
CNN ERM                  & 0.3494 & 0.3484 & 0.562 \\
CNN IRM                  & 0.3589 & 0.3438 & 0.448 \\
CNN GroupDRO             & 0.2605 & 0.2848 & 0.514 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig6_irm_comparison.pdf}
\caption{Invariance learning versus causal XGBoost on the
Chungbuk fold. Deep models collapse the
\texttt{normal}-class F1 (0.45--0.68) while XGBoost holds it
at 1.000, producing a persistent $-6.6$~pp macro-F1 gap
against causal XGBoost.\label{fig:irm}}
\end{figure}

A dedicated IRM hyperparameter sweep over
$\lambda_{\max} \in \{1, 10, 10^2, 10^3, 10^4\}$ and
$T_\mathrm{warmup} \in \{10, 30\}$ on the MLP found
$\lambda_{\max}=1$ to be the validation-selected setting
(both warmups tie), reaching test macro-F1~0.3145. The best
observed test configuration, $\lambda_{\max}=1000$ and
$T_\mathrm{warmup}=30$, reaches 0.3372 but is not selected by
validation. Both remain below causal-band XGBoost and
MLP~+~GroupDRO. On this dataset IRM is tunable but not transformative, a
finding consistent with~\cite{rosenfeld2021irm} and
\cite{gulrajani2021dg}.

The root cause of the deep-vs-tree gap is visible in the
per-class \texttt{normal} F1. An XGBoost tree trivially
separates \texttt{normal} from any-leak on a single split on
the MAX aggregates (overall spectrum energy), reaching F1
1.000 even on unseen regions. The class-balanced
cross-entropy loss used by the deep models, by contrast,
trades \texttt{normal} accuracy for marginal gains on the
four hard abnormal classes, causing the observed collapse.
This is not primarily a question of invariance; it is a
question of whether the \texttt{normal}-vs-abnormal
separation is being made along its natural low-dimensional
axis.

\subsection{Robust Causal-Band Identification}\label{sec:res-robust}

To harden the single-region causal-band finding of
Section~\ref{sec:res-perm}, the robustness protocol of
Section~\ref{sec:method-robust} is applied.
Table~\ref{tab:robust} reports bootstrap 95\% confidence
intervals on $\Delta_{\mathrm{F1}}$ per band group, replicated
across the four LORO folds with at least four leak classes and
at least 5{,}000 test rows.

