\subsection{Augmentation and Robust-Loss Recipes}\label{sec:res-aug}

Table~\ref{tab:aug} reports six training configurations on the
CNN baseline of Section~\ref{sec:method-irm} and an additional
MLP--causal configuration.

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{Training recipe sweep. SpecAugment delivers the
largest macro-F1 improvement (+2.0 pp over the CNN
baseline); focal loss mainly improves accuracy; Mixup hurts.\label{tab:aug}}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{lCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Recipe} & \textbf{Model} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{Accuracy} \\
\midrule
baseline (class-weighted CE) & CNN & 0.3341 & 0.3343 \\
\textbf{+ SpecAugment}       & \textbf{CNN} & \textbf{0.3544} & 0.3384 \\
+ Mixup                      & CNN & 0.3169 & 0.3431 \\
+ Focal loss ($\gamma$=2)    & CNN & 0.3393 & \textbf{0.3648} \\
all three combined           & CNN & 0.3226 & 0.3007 \\
Mixup + focal                & MLP (causal) & 0.2933 & 0.3147 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The strongest single-recipe CNN (SpecAugment) reaches
macro-F1 0.3544, +2.0 pp over the augmentation baseline and
still $-7.3$ pp below causal-band
XGBoost. This confirms that the CNN's deficit is not cured by
recipe engineering alone, although it is closed somewhat.

\subsection{Permutation-Guided Attention (Negative Result)}\label{sec:res-pga}

A permutation-importance prior injected into an attention
layer over spectrum bands, swept over $\lambda_c \in
\{0, 0.1, 1, 10, 100\}$ and uniform-vs-prior initialisation,
never becomes competitive in the test-fold diagnostic: the best
observed test macro-F1 is \textbf{0.3041} (at $\lambda_c=1$,
uniform init). This number is reported as a negative-result
ablation rather than as a validation-selected model. It is below
the plain MLP ERM baseline of Section~\ref{sec:res-irm} (0.3155)
and far below causal-band XGBoost (0.4273). The prior's value is diminished
because it is computed on training data where sensor overlap
is near-complete, so it inherits the shortcut bias it was
intended to overcome. The result reinforces
Section~\ref{sec:discussion}'s main conclusion: when a causal
feature is nameable a priori (here, the 121--422 Hz range),
hard feature selection is more reliable than any soft-prior
injection we tested.

\subsection{Hierarchical Classifier}\label{sec:res-hier}

The hierarchical recipe of Section~\ref{sec:method-hier}
targets exactly the \texttt{normal}-collapse failure mode.
Table~\ref{tab:hier} reports the results.

\begin{table}[H]
\caption{Hierarchical (binary energy gate $+$ 4-class causal
typer) versus flat 5-class XGBoost on the Chungbuk LORO
fold. Stage~1 achieves 100\% OOD accuracy on the
\texttt{normal} gate (45 of 45 normal rows detected, zero
false positives).\label{tab:hier}}
\begin{adjustwidth}{-\extralength}{0cm}
\begin{tabularx}{\fulllength}{lCCCCCCC}
\toprule
\textbf{Model} & \textbf{Acc} & \textbf{Macro-F1} & \textbf{in} & \textbf{out} & \textbf{noise} & \textbf{other} & \textbf{normal} \\
\midrule
Flat 5-class (causal)           & 0.3013 & 0.4273 & 0.326 & 0.206 & 0.391 & 0.214 & 1.000 \\
\textbf{Hierarchical (causal)}  & \textbf{0.3041} & \textbf{0.4287} & 0.345 & 0.200 & 0.377 & 0.222 & 1.000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{adjustwidth}
\end{table}

Stage~1 is perfect on OOD: every one of the 45 normal rows
in the Chungbuk test set is correctly flagged, with zero
false positives. Stage~2 operates on a cleaner training
distribution (abnormal-only, 43{,}189 rows), producing a
small but consistent macro-F1 gain and, more importantly for
field deployment, decoupling the alarm decision (Stage~1)
from the triage decision (Stage~2). For a field technician,
this separation is operationally meaningful: an alarm can be
dispatched on Stage~1's confidence alone, with Stage~2
producing a probable leak type to guide on-site investigation.

