%=================================================================
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\subsection{What the Benchmark Actually Supports}

Three positions follow from the results.

First, binary \texttt{normal}-vs-any-leak is a solved
problem on this benchmark. An XGBoost tree on the 20 MAX
aggregates achieves perfect OOD separation on the canonical
Chungbuk fold. This capability would plausibly transfer to
a field deployment and supports an operational alarm layer.

Second, 4-class abnormal typing is brittle. The highest OOD
macro-F1 observed under the pre-specified tree-only average is 0.4326,
followed by 0.4287 (hierarchical classifier) and 0.4273
(flat causal XGBoost); below these, the best invariance-
regularised MLP reaches 0.3615 and the augmented CNN 0.3544. The hardest class is
\texttt{other}: its OOD F1 ranges from 0.050 (anti-causal
XGBoost) to 0.222 (hierarchical, the best). The dataset's
environmental coverage is thin (49 sites in 12 regions, of
which only 1 has all five classes represented), and
definitive 4-class typing on new sites remains an open
problem.

Third, identifier-level features (\texttt{site},
\texttt{sid}, \texttt{lrate}, regional codes) should not be
used in any future benchmark on this dataset; doing so is
an evaluation mistake. The shortcut-audit protocol of
Section~\ref{sec:method-audit} can be run in a few minutes
and makes this diagnosis sharp.

\subsection{Why Invariance Learning Lags Causal Feature Selection}

The negative result of Section~\ref{sec:res-irm} deserves
emphasis. With the 57 causal features hand-picked and fed
to a well-regularised tree model, a 6.6~pp macro-F1 lead
over the best deep invariance-regularised alternative is
not closed by any amount of IRM or GroupDRO tuning in this
study.

Three factors explain the finding. First, the dataset has
only 11 training environments (regions), and they are
severely imbalanced in size (40$\times$ ratio between the
largest and smallest). IRM in particular is known to
destabilise under these
conditions~\cite{gulrajani2021dg,rosenfeld2021irm}. Second,
the class-balanced cross-entropy loss used by the deep
models trades the easy \texttt{normal}-vs-abnormal decision
for marginal gains on abnormal separation, giving up a
class that the tree model handles trivially. Third ---
perhaps most importantly for methodological practice --- the
causal spectral signature of leak noise can be named
\emph{ex ante} from the physics: plastic pipe leak noise is
broadly in the low hundreds of Hz, and the permutation-
importance diagnostic independently recovered exactly that
range. When the invariant feature is nameable from domain
knowledge, feature selection is both cheaper and more
reliable than loss-level regularisation.

This is a useful negative result for the broader sensor-ML
community: it suggests that invariance methods are best
reserved for problems where the invariant feature cannot be
named, such as raw imagery~\cite{beery2018terra}.

\subsection{Practical Recommendations for Practitioners}

For a utility or research group considering the AI~Hub pipeline
for field deployment, the audit delivers five concrete actions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Run the shortcut audit first.} Before training a
single model, run the four probes of
Section~\ref{sec:method-audit} on your split. If an \texttt{sid}
lookup plus a meta-column rule reproduces the target accuracy,
the benchmark result is identifier-driven and the headline
number is not a measurement of signal capability. Downstream
modelling effort is then better spent on redesigning the split
than on architecture search.

\item \textbf{Drop identifier columns during training.} On this
dataset \texttt{site}, \texttt{sid}, \texttt{lrate},
\texttt{llevel}, and all \texttt{region\_*n} codes should be
removed from the input. Under LORO evaluation, keeping them
provides zero accuracy benefit and slightly harms macro-F1
(Table~\ref{tab:matrix}).

\item \textbf{Restrict the spectrum to 65--422 Hz before
training the 4-class typer.} On the canonical Chungbuk fold
this raises macro-F1 from 0.4148 to 0.4273 and almost doubles
the F1 on the hardest (\texttt{other}) class. The 57-feature
causal subset also trains roughly ten times faster than the
full 534-feature set.

\item \textbf{Deploy two stages, not one.} Use the binary
normal-vs-abnormal gate (trained on all 57 causal features) to
raise a leak alarm, and consult the 4-class typer only when
the gate fires. On the Chungbuk fold, the gate achieves 100\%
OOD recall on the 45 normal rows with zero false positives;
the typer then works on a cleaner, abnormal-only distribution
and focuses attention on the hard decision (in/out/noise/other).

\item \textbf{Report LORO, not row-CV, as the evaluation of
record.} Row-level 10-fold cross-validation is a useful
internal debugging tool, but the number that matters for field
decisions is the LORO-weighted macro-F1 on the region with
the most balanced class distribution (Chungbuk here). On this
benchmark that number is currently 0.4273--0.4326 depending
on the recipe; any method that does not report this kind of
evaluation should be treated as producing an upper bound only.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Portability of the Methodology}

The shortcut-audit protocol, the three-tier OOD evaluation,
and the grouped permutation-importance diagnostic are
independent of the specific physics of water-pipe leaks.
They apply directly to any sensor-ML benchmark that has:
(i)~at least one identifier-like column, (ii)~a natural
hierarchy of group variables (sensor / site / region), and
(iii)~features that can be grouped physically (bands of a
spectrum, segments of a trajectory, channels of a
multivariate time series). Examples include gas-pipe leak
detection, rotating-machinery condition monitoring,
partial-discharge detection in power transformers, and
sound-based machine-fault diagnosis. Transferring the
protocol to those benchmarks is a natural next step.

\subsection{Limitations}

Three limitations are worth naming explicitly. First, the
causal-band identification is made on a single region (Chungbuk)
because no other fold contains all five classes in
substantial quantities. A larger, more geographically diverse
collection would test whether the 121--422 Hz range
transfers to other deployment contexts. Second, the
deep-learning architectures explored here are modest; a
Transformer-based spectrogram classifier or a self-
supervised pre-training pass might reduce the gap against
XGBoost. Third, the IRM sweep was confined to
$\lambda_{\max}$ and warm-up; tuning the MLP architecture
jointly with IRM might yield further improvements.

%=================================================================
\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusions}

This paper provides a field-deployment-oriented evaluation
of machine-learning water-leak detection on the Korean AI~Hub
vibration-sensor benchmark, to our knowledge the first to
apply sensor-, site-, and region-group holdouts to this
dataset. Four concrete
findings stand out. (1)~The published 99\%-range accuracies
on this benchmark are driven by identifier-label couplings
in the standard split; a four-rule lookup that ignores the
vibration spectrum reproduces 99.77\%. (2)~Under 12-fold
Leave-One-Region-Out, the same XGBoost classifier falls to
weighted 56.8\% accuracy and macro-F1 0.304 on 5-class leak
typing. (3)~On the only region containing all five classes
in substantial quantities, grouped permutation importance
localises the generalisable leak signal to the
121--422 Hz range, consistent with published leak-noise
acoustics for plastic pipes. (4)~A hierarchical classifier
combining a binary energy gate with a causal-band abnormal
typer, further combined through a pre-specified tree
soft-probability average, achieves OOD macro-F1
\textbf{0.4326} and perfect OOD normal-class recall, forming a
practical field-deployment candidate. Gradient-based invariance regularisation, evaluated
on two deep backbones and a dedicated hyperparameter sweep,
does not match causal feature selection. All code,
experiment logs, and figure-generation scripts are released
to enable further work.

